      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=1 - 3 e^{- x}$\n- **II.** $y=2 e^{- x}$\n- **III.** $y=-2 + 2 e^{- x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=1 - 3 e^{- x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 e^{- x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 3 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(1 - 3 e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ 3 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}1 - 3 e^{- x} \\\\ -1 + 6 e^{- x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=1 - 3 e^{- x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 e^{- x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 e^{- x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ - 2 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(2 e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ - 2 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}2 e^{- x} \\\\ - 4 e^{- x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 e^{- x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2 + 2 e^{- x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 e^{- x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ - 2 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}1\\cdot \\left(-2 + 2 e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ - 2 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 + 2 e^{- x} \\\\ 2 - 4 e^{- x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2 + 2 e^{- x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f23bfa81-8055-592a-b0c1-03502dcc8477",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
