  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fae4c2b8-7e66-5c61-8b42-ef461da6015f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=2 e^{x} + 2$\n- **II.** $y=1 + 2 e^{- x}$\n- **III.** $y=-1 + e^{- x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2 e^{x} + 2,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 e^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- y \\\\ 2 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1\\cdot \\left(2 e^{x} + 2\\right) \\\\ 2 e^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}- 2 e^{x} - 2 \\\\ 4 e^{x} + 2 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 e^{x} + 2$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=1 + 2 e^{- x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 e^{- x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- y \\\\ - 2 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1\\cdot \\left(1 + 2 e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ - 2 e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1 - 2 e^{- x} \\\\ 1 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=1 + 2 e^{- x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-1 + e^{- x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- e^{- x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- y \\\\ - e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}-1\\cdot \\left(-1 + e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ - e^{- x} & \\overset{?}{=}1 - e^{- x} \\\\ -1 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-1 + e^{- x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
